  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 200 PM CDT Thu Oct 8 2026\n\nIncrease in cloud cover Saturday due to Hurricane Isaias:\n\nCome the weekend, Hurricane Isaias is forecast to track\nsomewhere along the east side of the Mississippi River Valley,\nthen lift NE somewhere along the south side of the Ohio River\nValley. With high pressure aloft over our area, a sharp cutoff\nin the rain shield is forecast in our eastern areas. Therefore,\nsome cloud cover increase is expected east of Highway 65, but\nany precipitation would be rather light and sprinkly, bringing\nlittle relief for drought-stricken areas.\n\n\nHigh confidence in dry weather and above normal temperatures:\n\nAreas that don't see increased cloud cover Saturday are forecast\nto stay well-above normal. Highs continue in the mid-80s to\nlower 90s northwest of I-44. Those areas within the cloud cover\nhave forecasted highs within the 70s.\n\nOnce Isaias exits eastward, a more amplified ridge with an\nenergetic jet stream across the northern CONUS will continue to\nkeep our weather dry and borderline hot. Temperatures will\ncontinue to be in the mid-80s to lower 90s through the middle of\nnext week, with lows in the 60s. The hottest day looks to be\nMonday as the ridge axis moves over our area. Current forecasts\nare in the lower 90s, some areas nearing the middle 90s. This\nwill surely challenge the current records of 87 at West Plains,\n90 at Rolla-Vichy, 93 at Joplin, and 91 at Springfield.\n\nRelief from the well above normal temperatures may come later\nnext week as NBM spreads increase drastically. However, not much\nstock can be put into any one scenario due to the nature of the\nincoming pattern. Highs in the 80s to 90s could continue into\nnext weekend, or drop to the 70s by the end of the work week.\n\n\nUncertainty exists in 20-40% rain chances early next week:\n\nThe uncertainty in temperatures is due to high model spread in\nhow systems over the Pacific will interact next week. Tropical\nStorm Rachel, currently in the east Pacific Ocean, is forecast\nto move into the Desert Southwest on Sunday, merging with a \npolar jet stream trough over the west coast. This energy is then\nforecast to progress east-northeastward into the Plains next \nweek, which brings us our next 20-40% rain chances Tuesday into \nWednesday.\n\nThat said, LREF ensemble clusters are quite varied in the\nevolution of the pattern once Rachel gets absorbed into the jet\nstream. This evolution is much like the pattern that gave us our\nlast rain chances. One cluster has the tropical system quickly\nbeing absorbed and the wave quickly moving into our area between\nTuesday and Thursday. Another cluster has the polar trough\ndetach from the jet stream, become a closed low, and stay over\nthe southwest CONUS for much longer.\n\nIf you recall from the beginning of October, the closed low was\nthe scenario that panned out, and since models don't handle\nclosed lows well, the system continuously slowed down, pushing\nback rain chances. Given this scenario, would not be surprised\nto see a trend in later rain chances as we move forward and\nbetter resolve the features that will impact our weather. \n\nTime will tell, but whether its as early as Tuesday or as late\nas the end of the week, the large-scale pattern should bring a\nwave through sometime, which should cool things down and bring\nsome rain chances--eventually.",
